"Classification conjecture for perfect spanning sets | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/356749/classification-conjecture-for-perfect-spanning-sets)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Perfect spanning-set classification conjecture. ... Bela Bajnok, “A Walk Through Some Newer Parts of Additive Combinatorics”, arXiv:2211.01893 (2022).\n\n# \n\nClassification conjecture for perfect spanning sets\n\nLet $G$G be a finite abelian group, and let $A$A be a perfect spanning set of size $m$m and radius $s$s, meaning that every element of $G$G has a unique representation as a signed sum of at most $s$s elements of $A$A. Perfect spanning-set classification conjecture. The only instances of perfect spanning sets are the three cases described in the source: $s=1$s=1, arbitrary $m$m; $m=1$m=1, arbitrary $s$s; or $m=2$m=2, arbitrary $s$s. In particular, one must have $s=1$s=1 or $m\\in\\{1,2\\}$m∈{1,2}. The source lists constructions for these three cases and conjectures that no others exist; the general classification remains open.\n\nSources & references View supporting material\n\n## Primary source\n\nBela Bajnok, “A Walk Through Some Newer Parts of Additive Combinatorics”, arXiv:2211.01893 (2022).\n\nabelian-groupsadditive-combinatoricsbasis-problems\n\n0\n\n2 weeks ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1 Introduction (https://web.math.ucsb.edu/~casteels/GeisThesis.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.3 years ago; At a New York Number Theory Seminar in 2003, Bela Bajnok gave a talk titled *The Spanning Number and the Independence Number of a Subset of an Abelian Group* [2]. Although his focus was on general spanning sets and \\(s\\)-bases for abelian groups, he introduced the concept of a perfect \\(s\\)-basis of size \\(m\\). ... Then, we generalize the proof technique for Theorem 1.2 in order to prove more supporting results for the validity of Conjecture 1.1.\n1 Introduction\n\nLet \\(G\\) be a finite abelian group. Given an \\(s \\in \\mathbb{N}\\), a classical problem in additive combinatorics is determining the smallest \\(s\\)-basis for \\(G\\). Formally, finding an \\(m\\) such that\n\n\\[\nm=\\min\\{|A|:\\ A\\subseteq G \\text{ such that } [0,s]A=G\\}.\n\\]\n\nAt a New York Number Theory Seminar in 2003, Bela Bajnok gave a talk titled *The Spanning Number and the Independence Number of a Subset of an Abelian Group* [2]. Although his focus was on general spanning sets and \\(s\\)-bases for abelian groups, he introduced the concept of a perfect \\(s\\)-basis of size \\(m\\). Intuitively, these perfect \\(s\\)-bases are the smallest possible value of \\(m\\) where\n\n\\[\n[0,s]A \\le \\binom{m+s}{s}=|G|.\n\\]\n\nHe notes that he “could not find perfect spanning sets for \\(s \\ge 2\\) and \\(m \\ge 3\\).” Then in his 2017 book titled *Additive Combinatorics: A Menu of Research Problems* [1], he poses the following conjecture:\n\n**Conjecture 1.1.** *There are no perfect \\(s\\)-bases of size \\(m\\) for \\(G\\), unless \\(s=1\\) or \\(m=1\\).*\n\nAdditionally in [1], Bajnok proves that Conjecture 1.1 holds when \\(s=2,3\\).\n\n**Theorem 1.2.** *Conjecture 1.1 holds when \\(s=2,3\\).*\n\nThe main focus of this thesis will be attempting to prove Conjecture 1.1. In particular, we show that Conjecture 1.1 can only hold if \\(s<m\\). Then, we generalize the proof technique for Theorem 1.2 in order to prove more supporting results for the validity of Conjecture 1.1.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSubgroup perfect codes of $S_n$ in Cayley sum graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.05069)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; $ A subset $ C $ of $ G $ is called perfect code of $ G $ if there exists a Cayley sum graph of $ G $ that admits $ C $ as a perfect code. ... In this work, we prove that there does not exist any proper perfect subgroup code of symmetric group $ S_n $.\nTitle: Subgroup perfect codes of $S_n$ in Cayley sum graphs\nAuthors: Ankan Shaw, Biswajit Mondal, Satya Bagchi\nDate: Fri Sep  5 13:06:48 2025\n\nA perfect code in a graph $\\Gamma = (V, E)$ is a subset $C$ of $V$ such that no two vertices in $C$ are adjacent, and every vertex in $V \\setminus C$ is adjacent to exactly one vertex in $C$. Let $ G $ be a finite group, and let $ S $ be a square-free normal subset of $ G $. The Cayley sum graph of $ G $ with respect to $ S $ is a simple graph with vertex set $ G $ and two vertices $ x $ and $ y $ are adjacent if $ xy\\in S .$ A subset $ C $ of $ G $ is called perfect code of $ G $ if there exists a Cayley sum graph of $ G $ that admits $ C $ as a perfect code. In particular, if a subgroup of $ G $ is a perfect code of $ G $, then the subgroup is called a subgroup perfect code of $ G $. In this work, we prove that there does not exist any proper perfect subgroup code of symmetric group $ S_n $. Using this result, we provide a complete characterization of the perfect subgroup code of the alternating group $A_n$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSubgroup perfect codes of $S_n$ in Cayley sum graphs - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2509.05069v1)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.2 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; $ A subset $ C $ of $ G $ is called perfect code of $ G $ if there exists a Cayley sum graph of $ G $ that admits $ C $ as a perfect code. ... In this work, we prove that there does not exist any proper perfect subgroup code of symmetric group $ S_n $.\n\n## Subgroup perfect codes of $S_n$ in Cayley sum graphs\n\nAnkan Shaw, Biswajit Mondal, Satya Bagchi\n\nPublished: 2025-09-05\n\nComments: 18 pages\n\n\nPublication:\n\nalternating group graph group simple graph symmetric group\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nA perfect code in a graph $Γ= (V, E)$ is a subset $C$ of $V$ such that no two vertices in $C$ are adjacent, and every vertex in $V \\setminus C$ is adjacent to exactly one vertex in $C$. Let $ G $ be a finite group, and let $ S $ be a square-free normal subset of $ G $. The Cayley sum graph of $ G $ with respect to $ S $ is a simple graph with vertex set $ G $ and two vertices $ x $ and $ y $ are adjacent if $ xy\\in S .$ A subset $ C $ of $ G $ is called perfect code of $ G $ if there exists a Cayley sum graph of $ G $ that admits $ C $ as a perfect code. In particular, if a subgroup of $ G $ is a perfect code of $ G $, then the subgroup is called a subgroup perfect code of $ G $. In this work, we prove that there does not exist any proper perfect subgroup code of symmetric group $ S_n $. Using this result, we provide a complete characterization of the perfect subgroup code of the alternating group $A_n$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPerfect codes in Cayley graphs of abelian groups | Designs, Codes and Cryptography | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10623-026-01821-1)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last week; If either |H| or |G|/|H| is odd, then there exists a Cayley graph of G which admits H as a perfect code. ... $$\\begin{array}{r}{f}_{C}({x}_{1},\\dots ,{x}_{d}){f}_{{S}_{0}}({x}_{1},\\dots ,{x}_{d})=\\sum _{\\mathrm{\\varnothing }\\ne I\\subseteq \\{1,2,\\dots ,d\\}}\\sum _{g\\in G}{q}_{I}^{(g)}({x}_{1},\\dots ,{x}_{d})+\\prod _{i=1}^{d}\\left(\\sum _{j=0}^{{n}_{i}-1}{x}_{i}^{j}\\right).\\end{array}$$\n## 2 Preliminaries\n\nThe following lemma gives equivalent definitions of perfect codes and total perfect codes in Cayley graphs in terms of factorizations and tilings (see, for example, [7, 16]).\n\n### Lemma 2.1\n\nLet G be a group, S an inverse-closed subset of $G\\setminus \\{e\\}$ $G \\setminus \\{e\\}$, and C a subset of G.\n\n  1. (a)\n\nC is a perfect code in $\\mathrm{C}\\mathrm{a}\\mathrm{y}(G,S)$ $\\textrm{Cay}(G,S)$ if and only if $(S\\cup \\{e\\},C)$ $(S \\cup \\{e\\}, C)$ is a factorization of G (see [7]);\n\n  2. (b)\n\nC is a total perfect code in $\\mathrm{C}\\mathrm{a}\\mathrm{y}(G,S)$ $\\textrm{Cay}(G,S)$ if and only if (S, C) is a factorization of G.\n\nThus, if C is a perfect code (respectively, total perfect code) in $\\mathrm{C}\\mathrm{a}\\mathrm{y}(G,S)$ $\\textrm{Cay}(G, S)$, then $(|S|+1)|C|=|G|$ $(|S|+1) |C| = |G|$ (respectively, $|S||C|=|G|$ $|S| |C| = |G|$).\n\n### Lemma 2.2\n\n([16, Corollary 2.3]) Let G be a group and H a normal subgroup of G.\n\n  1. (a)\n\nIf either |H| or |G|/|H| is odd, then there exists a Cayley graph of G which admits H as a perfect code.\nNow suppose that $\\mathrm{C}\\mathrm{a}\\mathrm{y}(G,S)$ $\\textrm{Cay}(G,S)$ admits a perfect code C. Since $t=1$ $t = 1$ and l is the exponent of p in n, we have $n=a{p}^{l}$ $n = ap^l$ and ${n}_{1}={a}_{1}{p}^{l}$ $n_1 = a_1p^l$, where ${a}_{1}$ $a_1$ is not divisible by p. Then $a={a}_{1}{n}_{2}\\cdots {n}_{d}$ $a = a_1 n_2 \\cdots n_d$. By Lemma 4.1, for each pair (I, g), where $\\mathrm{\\varnothing }\\ne I\\subseteq \\{1,2,\\dots ,d\\}$ $\\emptyset \\ne I \\subseteq \\{1, 2, \\ldots , d\\}$ and $g=({g}_{1},\\dots ,{g}_{d})\\in G$ $g = (g_1, \\ldots , g_d) \\in G$, there exists a polynomial ${q}_{I}^{(g)}({x}_{1},\\dots ,{x}_{d})\\in \\mathbb{Z}[{x}_{1},\\dots ,{x}_{d}]$ $q_{I}^{(g)}(x_1, \\ldots , x_d) \\in \\mathbb {Z}[x_1, \\ldots , x_d]$ divisible by $(\\prod _{i=1}^{d}{x}_{i}^{{g}_{i}})(\\prod _{i\\in I}({x}_{i}^{{n}_{i}}-1))$ $(\\prod _{i=1}^d x_i^{g_i})(\\prod _{i \\in I}(x_i^{n_i} - 1))$ such that\n\n$$\\begin{array}{r}{f}_{C}({x}_{1},\\dots ,{x}_{d}){f}_{{S}_{0}}({x}_{1},\\dots ,{x}_{d})=\\sum _{\\ma